is a Ben Cohen Ben Cohen he's a big-time  sports reporter Wall Street Journal Ben  you might want to bail on this you might  not want to be affiliated with this it's  guilt by association but welcome to the  program author of the hot hand available  wherever books are sold we'll talk about  that coming up I noticed you've written  a little bit about the coronavirus where  where do you think the tipping point is  going to be sports-wise with the  coronavirus I think it's gonna be a  digital eye for a hot-dog-eating contest  no III I do think it's coming I mean to  me this whole thing is a bit like  watching the Titanic approach the  iceberg in slow motion like we know  what's going to happen we know it's  going to be very painful and yet people  on the deck haven't really wrap their  minds around it yet I don't think I mean  the idea of playing games and empty  stadiums was unrealistic not too long  ago but it's already become reality in  Asia and Europe and there's nothing  really about being in the u.s. that  inoculates us from this we need to be a  few weeks behind Asia in Europe I think  in terms of the spread of this virus and  so it would not really surprise me in  the least  to see extreme measures being enforced  here or pretty soon I think there's a  real possibility for severe disruptions  at like the busiest time of the calendar  for American sports but I also wonder  about this Ben the NCAA is slow-moving  you've got the final flower the final  Ford coming up in April and I wonder if  they would have the wherewithal to be  the first league that would make a  really dramatic decision and have these  basketball games played you know in in  front of empty arenas well I think the  Final Four is not the issue I mean  that's a hundred thousand people in a  football stadium but the thing about the  Final Four is that it's a month away and  I think that the NBA and the NHL will  probably have moved by then it seems  like they're probably going to move at  some point this week I mean it just  feels like the momentum is heading that  way so you know that the NCAA has to  worry about first-round games first  right I mean so that's next week I mean  there are conference tournaments this  weekend Vanderbilt has sent kids home  and and and canceled classes and yet the  SEC Tournament is is down the street in  Nashville so  the NCAA is not the most progressive  body I don't know if you were aware of  that and I think there will probably  have to be some pressure from local  authorities and states and maybe even  you know on the federal level and you  know I I don't know what they are going  to do I mean the idea of playing the  NCAA tournament  in empty arenas is very strange right  but it's a little bit different from the  NBA playoffs because these are neutral  site games anyway so there's not a home  court advantage that either team has I  think it would just be weird more than  anything I mentioned that you wrote the  book the hot hand it's available online  wherever books are sold and I was  fascinated by this because it was a  couple of decades ago I did a story at  ESPN it was called getting blue or  getting in the zone as athletes called  getting blue or in the zone and I  remember talking to Dennis Eckersley and  I said what's that feeling like and he  said I can't describe it he goes I don't  want to talk about it because I'll talk  myself out of it but you've written a  book about really getting in the zone  here the hot hand so explain to the  audience what the initiative or the  premise was behind writing this yeah so  you know there's really no singular  definition of the hot hand but I like to  think of it as when success leads to  more success and in basketball for  example it's always been studied in  basketball and that's when you make one  shot in a row and then another and then  another and you feel like you're going  to make your next shot right you can't  miss you're in the zone you're on fire  but what makes this really interesting  is that a few decades ago there was this  classic study about the hot hand and  what made it a classic was the  counterintuitive conclusion that there  is no such thing as the hot hand it  doesn't exist  it's simply seeing patterns in  randomness it was this cognitive bias  this easily digestable example of seeing  patterns and randomness and you know  once that study came out something kind  of amazing happened which is that nobody  believed it the athletes themselves  none of us because we'd all felt the hot  hand we'd seen the hot hand for  ourselves now some of this has changed  in recent years there are some evidence  to suggest that our intuition actually  may have been right all along but to me  like the reason I was so intrigued by  this was that it was a story that has  genius scholars and Nobel Prize winners  and NBA superstars  and it was really about something that  we all thought to be true only to be  told that it wasn't only to realize that  maybe it actually was and that was just  irresistible to me as a story but is  there such thing as home court advantage  like is that is that something is that  real where you have somebody who wins so  many games in a row at home well I think  yes that typically there are clearly  advantages to playing at home right  teams are are better at home this is a  little bit different right because we  all if this is sort of like personal  right it's it's individual it's not a  team thing and I think the real thing  here is one of control like there's  something amazing happens when we do  feel hot whether it's in basketball  whether it's in life I'm sure you felt  hot behind the mic sometimes right there  are just times when you're in a zone and  the question has always been like is  that real or is that an illusion and I  think it's something that we are still  trying to figure out I'm still thinking  about it even after spending all these  years writing about it but you have  athletes who try to put themselves in  the zone can you can you physically do  that I don't think so in fact I asked  Steph Curry about this because in the  book I write about the one night that  Steph Curry was the hottest he's ever  been was really a game that changed his  life and the fate of the Golden State  Warriors and the future of the entire  NBA and was a game against the Knicks in  2013 the most amazing part about it is  that the Knicks actually won the game  but no matter that I asked Steph like is  is there anything that you can do to put  yourself in the zone and he says he  doesn't know when it's going to happen  or where it's going to happen or how  it's going to happen or why it's going  to happen but once it does happen you  have to embrace it and he did that night  he scored 54 points he made 11 of his 13  threes it was kind of the game that  changed everything for him and for us  and for you know everybody who watches  basketball  what about gambling where you feel like  you're on a roll literally on a roll or  roulette or you know craps does it does  it play into that as well  um well we think it does something odd  happens when we walk into a casino when  we go to a basketball arena and Steph  Curry makes three shots in a row  everybody thinks that he's going to make  his fourth shot right that's the hot  hand when we walk into a casino and you  go to a roulette wheel and the ball  read three times in a row what research  has shown is that people bet on black  the next time that's the opposite of the  hot hand it's called the gamblers  fallacy and to me again this is about  control when we have agency of a  situation when we are the shooters and  when we are playing basketball we think  we can get hot but when we understand  that we were at the mercy of chance we  realize that we probably can't and so  the difference there is like a skilled  performance and random performance  skilled is when you can really take  advantage and random is when you  probably shouldn't and believing in the  hot hand can be disastrous it can  backfire on you and burn you a little  bit do you know more after you've done  this or less after you've done this that  is a great question I technically know  more about the hot hand but it sort of  made me question everything around me so  it's possible that I just spent like  three years becoming less smart about  something which is always a great way to  spend three years  give me the athlete that you would love  to talk to about this about the hot hand  well I you know it was Steph and I did  get to talk to him we saw LeBron talk  about it a few weeks ago after he made  five threes and like seven possessions  and he says of course the hot hand is  real which makes him like every other  NBA player and it actually might be klay  Thompson just because you never quite  know what's going to come out of Klay  Thompson's mouth and he I think like you  know I wrote about Steph but probably  the guy who embodies the hot hand is his  own teammate right like to score 37  points in a quarter is still an insane  achievement and I've watched that that  YouTube clip over and over and I know  those shots are going in and I still  have no idea how he actually does it so  I would love to like insert some truth  serum into him and just like just grill  Klay for like an hour about like what  that feels like then good luck with the  book we appreciate you joining us thank  you Dan that's Ben Cohen Wall Street  Journal sports reporter and author of  the hot hand yeah I remember Jordan
 

